# throwaway debug copy of the game, regenerated with instrumentation while testing
dbg.html
# screenshots dropped by the headless test scripts. These land beside the game at the
# repo root, so the rule is anchored with a leading slash — an unanchored *.png would
# also swallow every texture under Assets/, which Unity genuinely needs tracked.
/*.png
# playwright and its chromium are installed per-container, not vendored — package.json
# and the lockfile are committed so `npm install` reproduces the harness exactly.
node_modules/
# one-off measurement scripts. The game is served from the repo root, so a probe has to
# live here to import playwright at all — which makes it very easy to commit one. The
# .tmp.html rule is for the same reason: a page that loads a model on its own has to sit
# beside vendor/ to reach the loader, and "look at this model" is a throwaway every time.
probe*.tmp.mjs
*.tmp.html

# --- Unity ---
# Unity generates these and they are enormous — Library alone runs to gigabytes,
# and all of it is rebuilt from the files that ARE tracked. Committing any of it
# would make every pull a download of things your own machine can regenerate.
[Ll]ibrary/
[Tt]emp/
[Oo]bj/
[Bb]uild/
[Bb]uilds/
[Ll]ogs/
[Uu]ser[Ss]ettings/
[Mm]emoryCaptures/
[Rr]ecordings/

# the local caches Unity keeps beside the project
/.vs/
/.gradle/
/[Ee]xport[Cc]andidates/
*.csproj
*.unityproj
*.sln
*.suo
*.user
*.userprefs
*.pidb
*.booproj
*.svd
*.pdb
*.mdb
*.opendb
*.VC.db

# crash dumps and OS litter
sysinfo.txt
crashlytics-build.properties
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
_j.mjs
